 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the banking law, in relation to requiring a written\nnotification of overdraft fees charged to certain account holders",
    "basePrintNo": "S7202",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7202-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §9-y, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7202\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the banking law, in relation to requiring a written\nnotification of overdraft fees charged to certain account holders\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to require a bank to provide notice every\n180 days with a summary of all overdraft fees charged to an account and\ninformation on the account holder's ability to negotiate the overdraft\ncharges.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Adds section 9-y to the baking law, requiring that banking\ninstitutions provide an account holder with notice of overdraft fees,\nincluding:\n\n* dates and amounts of overdraft fees;\n\n* the total amount charged;\n\n* information on the customers ability to negotiate fees; and,\n\n* a telephone number and full contact information for a representative\nof the financial institution responsible for resolving any matter relat-\ning to such fee.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nOverdraft fees cost Americans $12.4 billion dollars in 2020 and the\naverage national overdraft fee was $24.38 per transaction. Often the\nbank will assess an account holder an overdraft fee instead of declining\nthe transaction, and while that may be helpful in covering expenses the\nresult undoubtedly causes further financial strain and hardship espe-\ncially to New Yorkers who live paycheck to paycheck.\n\nThis legislation will provide account holders with a clear summary of\ntheir overdraft fees and provide them with information on their ability\nto negotiate the fees with the bank, allowing account holders an oppor-\ntunity to recover some of their money.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nJanuary 1st, 2022\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
